    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.099.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "The thread through it all",
      "body": "A recurring pattern in Roman history is that whoever controlled the army controlled the state. After Marius, soldiers looked to their general, not the Senate, for pay and land. Sulla, Caesar, Vespasian and the emperors of the third century all rose on that loyalty."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.099.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "Roman history spans more than 2,000 years.",
      "context": "It runs from the traditional founding in 753 BCE to the fall of Constantinople in 1453 CE."
    },
